at he is actually Michelle, also a zerophiliac. They make love repeatedly, changing sex mostly in sync with each other, but occasionally finding themselves the same sex. It ends with the happy couple apparently resolved to their 'condition' and past any questions of their sexual identity.\n\nQuestion: Which character earned Luke's trust then tricked him into helping them switch genders?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Dr. Sydney Catchadourian earned Luke's trust and tricked him into helping her switch genders. She convinced him that having sex with another zerophiliac would make them both a-morphic, but she used him to change into a man herself. This left Luca as a woman, revealing her deception.\n\n\\boxed{Dr. Sydney Catchadourian}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 583, "output_tokens": 75, "total_tokens": 658}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 4.8892999999999994e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9563", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  \"Zerophilia\" is a fictional condition that affects an unknown number of people with an extra \"Z\" chromosome. Following their first full sexual experience, zerophiliacs begin to change sex after experiencing an orgasm. Luke (Taylor Handley), a young man somewhat insecure about his masculinity, begins to exhibit zerophilia following an encounter with a woman (Kelly Le Brock). He meets Michelle (Rebecca Mozo) and experiences partial transformations when they go out together.\nHe confides with his best friend Keenan (Dustin Seavey) about his partial transformations, who in turn contacts Dr. Sydney Catchadourian (Gina Bellman). Dr. Catchadourian persuades Luke to go through a full transformation. Luke does this by masturbating, becoming female, and subsequently calling herself \"Luca\". Luca has difficulty achieving an orgasm to change back, even with coaching from Keenan's girlfriend Janine (Alison Folland). However, a visit by Michelle's attractive brother, Max (Kyle Schmid), who flirts with \"Luke's cousin\", enables her to get sufficiently aroused to complete the transformation back to Luke.\nLuke is threatened by his sex transformation, his arousal by an attractive male, and the questions of sexual identity it raises; he seeks help from Sydney. She tells him that a zerophiliac can become \"a-morphic\" and stop changing sex only by having sex with another zerophiliac... such as herself. He reluctantly agrees to do it, but discovers afterward that she was not telling him the full truth: an a-morphic zerophiliac can still change by having sex with another zerophiliac, and Dr. Catchadourian was using Luke to change herself one last time (into a man), leaving Luca as a woman in the process.\nComic tensions arise from Luke's efforts to keep Michelle at a distance, Max's defensiveness about his sister, Luca's half-hearted resistance to Max's affections, and Luke's confused aggression toward Max. When Michelle discovers that Luke had sex with Dr. Catchadourian, she feels betrayed. Hoping to find Michelle, Luca seeks out Max to profess deep affection for Michelle and remorse for betraying her. Max is touched by the apology, and reveals that he is actually Michelle, also a zerophiliac. They make love repeatedly, changing sex mostly in sync with each other, but occasionally finding themselves the same sex. It ends with the happy couple apparently resolved to their 'condition' and past any questions of their sexual identity.\n\nQuestion: What did Luke do to fully transition into Luca for the first time?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Luke fully transitioned into Luca (a female) after Dr. Catchadourian persuaded him to experience a complete transformation. Per the plot summary, he achieved this by masturbating to orgasm, after which he began referring to himself as Luca. (Partial shifts had occurred earlier, but this was the first full change.)\n\n\\boxed{masturbated (to full orgasm)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 756, "output_tokens": 217, "total_tokens": 973}, "provider": "xAI", "model_used": "x-ai/grok-4.3", "requested_model": "x-ai/grok-4.3", "invoked_at": "2026-08-05T16:35:30.031467+00:00"}, "cost": 0.0014875, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9565", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  \"Zerophilia\" is a fictional condition that affects an unknown number of people with an extra \"Z\" chromosome. Following their first full sexual experience, zerophiliacs begin to change sex after experiencing an orgasm. Luke (Taylor Handley), a young man somewhat insecure about his masculinity, begins to exhibit zerophilia following an encounter with a woman (Kelly Le Brock). He meets Michelle (Rebecca Mozo) and experiences partial transformations when they go out together.\nHe confides with his best friend Keenan (Dustin Seavey) about his partial transformations, who in turn contacts Dr. Sydney Catchadourian (Gina Bellman). Dr. Catchadourian persuades Luke to go through a full transformation. Luke does this by masturbating, becoming female, and subsequently calling herself \"Luca\". Luca has difficulty achieving an orgasm to change back, even with coaching from Keenan's girlfriend Janine (Alison Folland). However, a visit by Michelle's attractive brother, Max (Kyle Schmid), who flirts with \"Luke's cousin\", enables her to get sufficiently aroused to complete the transformation back to Luke.\nLuke is threatened by his sex transformation, his arousal by an attractive male, and the questions of sexual identity it raises; he seeks help from Sydney. She tells him that a zerophiliac can become \"a-morphic\" and stop changing sex only by having sex with another zerophiliac... such as herself. He reluctantly agrees to do it, but discovers afterward that she was not telling him the full truth: an a-morphic zerophiliac can still change by having sex with another zerophiliac, and Dr. Catchadourian was using Luke to change herself one last time (into a man), leaving Luca as a woman in the process.\nComic tensions arise from Luke's efforts to keep Michelle at a distance, Max's defensiveness about his sister, Luca's half-hearted resistance to Max's affections, and Luke's confused aggression toward Max. When Michelle discovers that Luke had sex with Dr. Catchadourian, she feels betrayed. Hoping to find Michelle, Luca seeks out Max to profess deep affection for Michelle and remorse for betraying her. Max is touched by the apology, and reveals that he is actually Michelle, also a zerophiliac. They make love repeatedly, changing sex mostly in sync with each other, but occasionally finding themselves the same sex. It ends with the happy couple apparently resolved to their 'condition' and past any questions of their sexual identity.\n\nQuestion: Which extra chromosome do people have that are afflicted with zerophilia?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "People afflicted with zerophilia have an extra \"Z\" chromosome. This fictional condition triggers sex changes after orgasm, as depicted in the narrative. The extra chromosome is specifically referred to as the \"Z\" chromosome.\n\n\\boxed{Z}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 580, "output_tokens": 48, "total_tokens": 628}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 4.5980000000000004e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9566", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  \"Zerophilia\" is a fictional condition that affects an unknown number of people with an extra \"Z\" chromosome. Following their first full sexual experience, zerophiliacs begin to change sex after experiencing an orgasm. Luke (Taylor Handley), a young man somewhat insecure about his masculinity, begins to exhibit zerophilia following an encounter with a woman (Kelly Le Brock). He meets Michelle (Rebecca Mozo) and experiences partial transformations when they go out together.\nHe confides with his best friend Keenan (Dustin Seavey) about his partial transformations, who in turn contacts Dr. Sydney Catchadourian (Gina Bellman). Dr. Catchadourian persuades Luke to go through a full transformation. Luke does this by masturbating, becoming female, and subsequently calling herself \"Luca\". Luca has difficulty achieving an orgasm to change back, even with coaching from Keenan's girlfriend Janine (Alison Folland). However, a visit by Michelle's attractive brother, Max (Kyle Schmid), who flirts with \"Luke's cousin\", enables her to get sufficiently aroused to complete the transformation back to Luke.\nLuke is threatened by his sex transformation, his arousal by an attractive male, and the questions of sexual identity it raises; he seeks help from Sydney. She tells him that a zerophiliac can become \"a-morphic\" and stop changing sex only by having sex with another zerophiliac... such as herself. He reluctantly agrees to do it, but discovers afterward that she was not telling him the full truth: an a-morphic zerophiliac can still change by having sex with another zerophiliac, and Dr. Catchadourian was using Luke to change herself one last time (into a man), leaving Luca as a woman in the process.\nComic tensions arise from Luke's efforts to keep Michelle at a distance, Max's defensiveness about his sister, Luca's half-hearted resistance to Max's affections, and Luke's confused aggression toward Max. When Michelle discovers that Luke had sex with Dr. Catchadourian, she feels betrayed. Hoping to find Michelle, Luca seeks out Max to profess deep affection for Michelle and remorse for betraying her. Max is touched by the apology, and reveals that he is actually Michelle, also a zerophiliac. They make love repeatedly, changing sex mostly in sync with each other, but occasionally finding themselves the same sex. It ends with the happy couple apparently resolved to their 'condition' and past any questions of their sexual identity.\n\nQuestion: What happens to a zerophiliac after their first sexual encounter?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Zerophiliacs begin changing sex after orgasms once they have had their first full sexual experience. This is triggered by the extra \"Z\" chromosome and leads to repeated transformations (partial or complete) thereafter, as seen with Luke/Luca. The condition can eventually be stabilized only through sex with another zerophiliac.\n\n\\boxed{they start changing sex after each orgasm}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 694, "output_tokens": 77, "total_tokens": 771}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00106, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_958", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  In 1940, Carl Fredricksen is a young 9-year-old boy who idolizes famous explorer Charles Muntz. Muntz has been accused of fabricating the skeleton of a giant exotic bird he says he discovered at Paradise Falls, and vows to return there to catch one alive. One day, Carl befriends a girl named Ellie, who is also a fan of Muntz. She confides to Carl her desire to move her \"clubhouse\"\u00e2\u0080\u0094an abandoned house in the neighborhood\u00e2\u0080\u0094to a cliff overlooking Paradise Falls. Carl and Ellie eventually get married and live together in the restored house. Carl sells toy balloons from a cart at a zoo Ellie opens. After suffering a miscarriage and being told they cannot have a child, the two decide to realize their dream of visiting Paradise Falls. They try to save for the trip, but repeatedly end up spending the money on more pressing needs. Finally, an elderly Carl arranges for the trip, but Ellie suddenly becomes ill and dies.\nYears later, Carl still lives in the house, stubbornly holding out as the surrounding neighborhood is torn down for new construction, but when he accidentally injures a construction worker over damage to his mailbox, a court orders him to move to a retirement home. However, Carl comes up with a scheme to keep his promise to Ellie, and turns his house into a makeshift airship, using thousands of helium balloons. Russell, a young Wilderness Explorer, becomes an accidental stowaway in his effort to earn his final merit badge for assisting the elderly. After surviving a thunderstorm, the flying house lands on a tepui opposite Paradise Falls. Carl and Russell harness themselves to the still-buoyant house and begin to walk it across the mesa, hoping to reach the falls before the balloons deflate. Russell encounters a tall, colorful flightless bird, whom he names \"Kevin\". They then meet a Golden Retriever named Dug, who wears a special collar that allows him to speak, and who vows to take the bird to his master.\nThe group is set upon by a pack of aggressive dogs led by Alpha, a doberman (who is also seeking the bird), and are taken to their master, who turns out to be an elderly Charles Muntz. Muntz invites Carl and Russell aboard his dirigible, where he explains that he has spent years since his disgrace searching for the giant bird. When Russell notes the bird's similarity to Kevin, Muntz becomes hostile, believing they have been attempting to steal the bird. The pair flees with Kevin and Dug, but Muntz catches up with them, captures Kevin and starts a fire beneath Carl's house, forcing him to choose between saving it or Kevin. Carl rushes to put out the fire, allowing Muntz to take the bird. He and Russell eventually reach the falls, though Russell is disappointed in Carl over his decision to abandon Kevin.\nSettling into his home, Carl looks through Ellie's childhood scrapbook, and is surprised to find that she has filled in the blank pages with photos of their marriage, along with a note thanking him for the \"adventure\" and encouraging him to go have a new one. Reinvigorated, he goes to find Russell, only to see him sailing off with some balloons to save Kevin on his own. Carl empties the house of furniture and possessions, lightening it, and pursues him. Russell is captured by Muntz, but Carl manages to board the dirigible in flight and free both him and Kevin. Dug accidentally defeats Alpha and becomes the dogs' new leader. Muntz pursues them around the airship, finally cornering Dug, Kevin, and Russell inside Carl's tethered house. Carl lures Kevin back onto the airship with Dug and Russell clinging to her back, but when Muntz leaps after them, he snags his foot on some balloon lines and falls to his death. The house then descends out of sight through the clouds.\nCarl and Russell reunite Kevin with her chicks, then fly the dirigible back to the city. Carl presents Russell with his final badge: a grape soda cap that Ellie gave to Carl when they first met and made their promise. The two and Dug then enjoy some ice cream together. Meanwhile, Carl's house has landed on the cliff beside Paradise Falls, fulfilling his promise to Ellie.\n\nQuestion: What allowed Dug to speak?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The context directly states that Dug \"wears a special collar that allows him to speak.\" This is the sole explanation given for his ability (distinct from the other dogs, who are not described as speaking). No other mechanism or detail is provided.\n\n\\boxed{special collar (w/ translator)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1054, "output_tokens": 59, "total_tokens": 1113}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.001465, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9585", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  In 1959, Alfred Hitchcock opens his latest film, North by Northwest, to considerable success, but is troubled by a reporter's insinuation that it is time to retire. Seeking to reclaim the artistic daring of his youth, Hitchcock turns down film proposals like adapting Casino Royale in favor of a horror novel called Psycho by Robert Bloch, which is based on the crimes of murderer Ed Gein. Gein appears in sequences throughout the film in which he seems to prompt Hitchcock's imagination regarding the Psycho story, or act as some function of Hitchcock's subconscious mind (for instance, drawing Hitchcock's attention to sand on his bathroom floor, the quantity of which reveals how much time his wife Alma has been spending at the beachhouse with Whitfield Cook).\nHitchcock's wife and artistic collaborator, Alma, is no more enthusiastic about the idea than his colleagues, especially since she is being lobbied by their writer friend, Whitfield Cook, to look at his own screenplay. However, she warms to Hitchcock's proposal, suggesting the innovative plot turn of killing the female lead early in the film. The studio heads at Paramount prove more difficult to persuade, forcing Hitchcock to finance the film personally and use his Alfred Hitchcock Presents television crew (over at competitor Revue/Universal) to produce the film. (As this film completed his contract with Paramount, all subsequent films were made at Universal.)\nHowever, the pressures of the production, such as dealing with Geoffrey Shurlock of the Motion Picture Production Code, and Hitchcock's lecherous habits, such as when they confer with the female lead, Janet Leigh, annoy Alma. She begins a personal writing collaboration with Whitfield Cook on his screenplay at his beach house without Hitchcock's knowledge. Hitchcock eventually discovers what she has been doing and suspects her of having an affair. This concern affects Hitchcock's work on Psycho. Hitchcock eventually confronts Alma and asks her if she is having an affair. Alma angrily denies it.\nAlma temporarily takes over production of the film when Hitchcock is bedridden after collapsing from overwork, but this sequence, which included a complicated process shot showing Arbogast's demise, with Alma's specification of a 35mm lens, instead of the 50mm lens preferred by Hitchcock for this film, proved to be the least effective in the film.\nMeanwhile, Hitchcock expresses his disappointment to Vera Miles at how she didn't follow through on his plan to make her the next biggest star after Grace Kelly; but Miles says she is happy with her family life.\nHitchcock's cut of Psycho is poorly received by the studio executives, while Alma discovers Whitfield having sex with a younger woman at his beach house. Hitchcock and Alma reconcile and set to work on improving the film. Their renewed collaboration yields results, culminating in Alma convincing Hitchcock to accept their composer's suggestion for adding Bernard Herrmann's harsh strings score to the shower scene.\nAfter maneuvering Shurlock into leaving the film's content largely intact, Hitchcock learns the studio is only going to exhibit the film in two theaters. Hitchcock arranges for special theater instructions to pique the public's interest such as forbidding admittance after the film begins. At the film's premiere, Hitchcock first views the audience from the projection booth, looking out through its small window at the audience (a scene which recalls his spying on his leading actresses undressing earlier in the film\u00e2\u0080\u0093by looking through a hole cut in the dressing room wall\u00e2\u0080\u0093which itself is a voyeuristic motif included in the film of Psycho). Hitchcock then waits in the lobby for the audience's reaction, conducting slashing motions to their reactions as they scream on cue. The film is rewarded with an enthusiastic reception.\nWith the film's screening being so well received, Hitchcock publicly thanks his wife afterward for helping make it possible and they affirm their love. At the conclusion at his home, Hitchcock addresses the audience noting Psycho proved a major high point of his career and he is currently pondering his next project. A raven lands on his shoulder as a reference to The Birds, before turning to meet with his wife.\nThe final title cards say that Hitchcock directed six more films after Psycho, none of which would eclipse its commercial success, and although he never won an Oscar, the American Film Institute awarded him its Life Achievement Award in 1979 - an award he claimed he shared, as he had his life, with his wife, Alma.\n\nQuestion: What does the reporter insinuate that troubles Alfred?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The context opens by stating that Hitchcock is \"troubled by a reporter's insinuation that it is time to retire\" after the success of North by Northwest. This directly prompts his decision to pursue a daring new project like Psycho rather than safer options. No other insinuation by the reporter is mentioned.\n\n\\boxed{it's time to retire}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1055, "output_tokens": 68, "total_tokens": 1123}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00148875, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9626", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  As a child, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) had been introduced to organized crime by Irish-American mobster Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) in the Irish neighborhood of South Boston. Over the years, Costello grooms him to become a mole inside the Massachusetts State Police, until Sullivan is accepted into the Special Investigations Unit, which focuses on organized crime.\nBefore graduating from the police academy, Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is recruited by Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sergeant Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) to go undercover, as his family ties to organized crime make him a perfect infiltrator. He drops out of the academy and does time in prison on a fake assault charge to increase his credibility.\nEach man infiltrates his respective organization, and Sullivan begins a romance with police psychiatrist Madolyn Madden (Vera Farmiga). Costigan is seeing her under the terms of his probation, and they begin a relationship, too. After Costello escapes a sting operation, each mole becomes aware of the other's existence. Sullivan is told to find the \"rat\" and asks Costello for information to identify the informer.\nCostigan follows Costello into a porn theater, where Costello gives Sullivan an envelope containing personal information on his crew members. Costigan chases Sullivan through Chinatown. When it is over, neither man knows the other's identity. Sullivan has Queenan tailed to a meeting with Costigan on the roof of a building. Queenan orders Costigan to flee while he confronts Costello's men alone. The men then throw Queenan off the building to his death. When they exit, Costigan pretends he has come to join them. Television news reports that crew member Delahunt (Mark Rolston) has been a Boston Police Department undercover cop, but Costello believes it to be a lie probably designed to lull him into a sense of security. Dignam resigns rather than work with Sullivan, who he suspects is the mole after he is asked why he had Queenan followed.\nUsing Queenan's phone, Sullivan reaches Costigan, who refuses to abort his mission. Sullivan learns from Queenan's diary of Costello's role as an informant for the FBI, causing him to worry about his own identity being revealed. With Costigan's help, Costello is traced to a cocaine drop-off, where a gunfight erupts between Costello's crew and the police, which results in most of the crew being killed. Costello, confronted by Sullivan, admits he is an FBI informant. Costello tries to shoot Sullivan, but Sullivan shoots him multiple times. With Costello dead, Sullivan is applauded the next day by everyone on the force. In good faith, Costigan comes to Sullivan for restoration of his true identity and to be paid for his work, but notices the envelope from Costello on Sullivan's desk and flees, finally realizing Sullivan is the enemy. Fearing retaliation, Sullivan erases Costigan's records from the police computer system.\nSullivan is unaware that Madolyn had an affair with Costigan when she tells Sullivan that she is pregnant. Later, Sullivan finds her listening to a CD from Costigan containing incriminating recorded conversations between Costello and Sullivan. Sullivan unsuccessfully attempts to assuage her suspicions. He then contacts Costigan, who reveals that Costello recorded every one of their conversations, that Costello's attorney left Costigan in possession of the recordings, and that Costigan intends to implicate Sullivan. The two agree to meet at the building where Queenan died.\nOn the roof, Costigan catches Sullivan off-guard and handcuffs him. As Costigan had secretly arranged, Trooper Brown (Anthony Anderson) appears on the roof as well. Shocked, Brown draws his gun on Costigan, who attempts to justify his actions by exposing Sullivan as Costello's mole. Costigan asks Brown why Dignam did not accompany him as Costigan had requested, but Brown does not answer. Costigan leads Sullivan, his hostage, to the elevator. When it reaches the ground floor, Trooper Barrigan (James Badge Dale) shoots Costigan in the head, then shoots Brown, and afterward reveals to Sullivan that Costello had more than one mole in the police. Sullivan then shoots and kills Barrigan. At state police headquarters, Sullivan identifies Barrigan as the mole and recommends Costigan for the Medal of Merit.\nAt Costigan's funeral, Sullivan notices that Madolyn is tearful. As they leave the gravesite Sullivan attempts to talk to her but she ignores him. When Sullivan returns to his apartment, he is ambushed by Dignam, who shoots and kills him as he enters.\n\nQuestion: What does Costello groom Colin for?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Costello grooms Colin Sullivan from childhood to serve as an inside man (or \"mole\") within the Massachusetts State Police. The explicit purpose is to enable Sullivan to infiltrate the force's Special Investigations Unit and provide information on organized-crime investigations. This arrangement is described in the opening paragraph of the supplied context and is the foundation for all later plot events.\n\n\\boxed{to be a mole in the MA State Police (SIU)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1097, "output_tokens": 87, "total_tokens": 1184}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00158875, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9682", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  Stuart \"Stu\" Shepard (Colin Farrell) is an arrogant New York City publicist who has been courting a woman named Pam (Katie Holmes) behind his wife Kelly (Radha Mitchell). He uses the last remaining public phone booth in the city to contact Pam. During the call, he is interrupted by a pizza delivery man, who attempts to deliver a free pizza to him, but Stu rudely turns him away by insulting his weight. As soon as Stu completes his call to Pam, the phone rings. Stu answers, to find that The Caller, who knows his name, warns him not to leave the booth, and says he will say hello to Pam for him. He also says he will call Kelly, leaving Stu panicked.\nThe caller tells Stu that he has tested two previous individuals who have done wrong deeds in a similar manner (one was a pedophile, the other was a company insider who cashed out his stock options before the share price collapsed), giving each a chance to reveal the truth to those they wronged, but in both cases, neither agreed and were killed. To demonstrate the threat, the caller fires a suppressed sniper rifle at a toy robot sold by a nearby vendor; the damage is unseen by anyone but Stu, the caller, and the vendor. The caller demands that Stu confess his feelings for Pam to both Kelly and Pam to avoid being killed. The caller contacts Pam, and puts her on line with Stu, who reveals that he is married. The caller then hangs up, telling Stu to call Kelly himself.\nAs Stu hesitates, the booth is approached by three prostitutes demanding to use the phone. Stu refuses to leave, having been warned by the caller to stay in the booth and not reveal the situation. Leon (John Enos III), the prostitutes' pimp, joins his charges, smashes the side of the booth, grabs at Stu in a headlock and starts punching him. The caller offers to \"make him stop\" and asks if Stu can hear him, which Stu just answers positively, causing the caller to misunderstand Stu and shoot Leon. Leon staggers away before collapsing dead in the street. The prostitutes immediately blame Stu, making a scene over Leon's body, accusing him of having a gun as the police and news crews converge on the location.\nPolice Captain Ed Ramey (Forest Whitaker), already suspecting Stu of being the killer, seals off the streets with police roadblocks and starts trying to negotiate to get him to leave the booth, but Stu refuses, telling the caller that there is no way they can incriminate him; the caller proves him wrong, calling his attention to a handgun that was planted in the roof of the phone booth. Both Kelly and Pam soon arrive on the scene. The caller demands that Stu tell Kelly the truth, which he does. The caller then orders Stu to choose between Kelly and Pam, and the woman he does not choose will be killed.\nWhile on the phone with the caller, Stu secretly uses his cell phone to call Kelly, allowing her to overhear his conversation with the caller. She, in turn, quietly informs Captain Ramey of this. Meanwhile, Stu continues to confess to everyone that his whole life is a lie, to make himself look more important than he really is or even feels. Stu's confession provides sufficient distraction to allow the police to trace the payphone call to a nearby building, and Ramey uses coded messages to inform Stu of this. Stu warns the caller that the police are on the way, and the caller replies that if he is caught, then he will kill Kelly. Panicked, Stu grabs the handgun and leaves the booth, screaming for the sniper to kill him instead of Kelly. The police fire upon Stu, while a smaller force breaks into the room that the caller was tracked to, only to find the gun and a man's corpse.\nStu regains consciousness to find the police fired only rubber bullets at him, stunning but not harming him. Stu and Kelly happily reunite. As the police bring down the body, Stu identifies it as the pizza delivery man from earlier. Stu gets medical treatment at a local ambulance; as he does, a man with a briefcase (Kiefer Sutherland) passes by and says that he regrets killing the pizza deliverer and warns Stu that if his new-found honesty does not last, he will be hearing from him again. The man disappears into the crowd with Stu unable to call out due to being sedated by the paramedics. As he does, someone else is being called from that same line. The audience could only hear him say, \"Hello?\" and the film ends.\n\nQuestion: What wrong deed has Stu done?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Stu\u2019s primary misdeed, as identified by the Caller, is his ongoing infidelity: he is secretly courting Pam while remaining married to Kelly and has repeatedly lied to both women (and others) to conceal the affair and inflate his own importance.  \n\nThis parallels the Caller\u2019s earlier \u201ctests\u201d of a pedophile and an insider-trader, with the demand that Stu publicly confess the truth to avoid being killed.  \n\nSecondary behaviors (rudeness to the pizza delivery man, habitual self-aggrandizing lies) are noted but are treated as ancillary to the central wrongdoing of marital deception.\n\n\\boxed{cheating on wife (w/ Pam; + habitual lies)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1111, "output_tokens": 134, "total_tokens": 1245}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00172375, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9704", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  Stuart \"Stu\" Shepard (Colin Farrell) is an arrogant New York City publicist who has been courting a woman named Pam (Katie Holmes) behind his wife Kelly (Radha Mitchell). He uses the last remaining public phone booth in the city to contact Pam. During the call, he is interrupted by a pizza delivery man, who attempts to deliver a free pizza to him, but Stu rudely turns him away by insulting his weight. As soon as Stu completes his call to Pam, the phone rings. Stu answers, to find that The Caller, who knows his name, warns him not to leave the booth, and says he will say hello to Pam for him. He also says he will call Kelly, leaving Stu panicked.\nThe caller tells Stu that he has tested two previous individuals who have done wrong deeds in a similar manner (one was a pedophile, the other was a company insider who cashed out his stock options before the share price collapsed), giving each a chance to reveal the truth to those they wronged, but in both cases, neither agreed and were killed. To demonstrate the threat, the caller fires a suppressed sniper rifle at a toy robot sold by a nearby vendor; the damage is unseen by anyone but Stu, the caller, and the vendor. The caller demands that Stu confess his feelings for Pam to both Kelly and Pam to avoid being killed. The caller contacts Pam, and puts her on line with Stu, who reveals that he is married. The caller then hangs up, telling Stu to call Kelly himself.\nAs Stu hesitates, the booth is approached by three prostitutes demanding to use the phone. Stu refuses to leave, having been warned by the caller to stay in the booth and not reveal the situation. Leon (John Enos III), the prostitutes' pimp, joins his charges, smashes the side of the booth, grabs at Stu in a headlock and starts punching him. The caller offers to \"make him stop\" and asks if Stu can hear him, which Stu just answers positively, causing the caller to misunderstand Stu and shoot Leon. Leon staggers away before collapsing dead in the street. The prostitutes immediately blame Stu, making a scene over Leon's body, accusing him of having a gun as the police and news crews converge on the location.\nPolice Captain Ed Ramey (Forest Whitaker), already suspecting Stu of being the killer, seals off the streets with police roadblocks and starts trying to negotiate to get him to leave the booth, but Stu refuses, telling the caller that there is no way they can incriminate him; the caller proves him wrong, calling his attention to a handgun that was planted in the roof of the phone booth. Both Kelly and Pam soon arrive on the scene. The caller demands that Stu tell Kelly the truth, which he does. The caller then orders Stu to choose between Kelly and Pam, and the woman he does not choose will be killed.\nWhile on the phone with the caller, Stu secretly uses his cell phone to call Kelly, allowing her to overhear his conversation with the caller. She, in turn, quietly informs Captain Ramey of this. Meanwhile, Stu continues to confess to everyone that his whole life is a lie, to make himself look more important than he really is or even feels. Stu's confession provides sufficient distraction to allow the police to trace the payphone call to a nearby building, and Ramey uses coded messages to inform Stu of this. Stu warns the caller that the police are on the way, and the caller replies that if he is caught, then he will kill Kelly. Panicked, Stu grabs the handgun and leaves the booth, screaming for the sniper to kill him instead of Kelly. The police fire upon Stu, while a smaller force breaks into the room that the caller was tracked to, only to find the gun and a man's corpse.\nStu regains consciousness to find the police fired only rubber bullets at him, stunning but not harming him. Stu and Kelly happily reunite. As the police bring down the body, Stu identifies it as the pizza delivery man from earlier. Stu gets medical treatment at a local ambulance; as he does, a man with a briefcase (Kiefer Sutherland) passes by and says that he regrets killing the pizza deliverer and warns Stu that if his new-found honesty does not last, he will be hearing from him again. The man disappears into the crowd with Stu unable to call out due to being sedated by the paramedics. As he does, someone else is being called from that same line. The audience could only hear him say, \"Hello?\" and the film ends.\n\nQuestion: Stu had to choose between Kelly and Pam, what would happen then?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The caller explicitly states that Stu must choose between Kelly and Pam, after which the woman not selected will be killed by sniper fire. This demand is part of the ongoing test/ultimatum, mirroring the caller's prior killings of those who refused to confess their wrongs. Stu never actually makes the choice, as he instead exposes the situation via his cell phone and attempts to sacrifice himself, allowing the police to intervene.\n\n\\boxed{caller kills the unchosen woman (by sniper)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1118, "output_tokens": 96, "total_tokens": 1214}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.0016375, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9707", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  Stuart \"Stu\" Shepard (Colin Farrell) is an arrogant New York City publicist who has been courting a woman named Pam (Katie Holmes) behind his wife Kelly (Radha Mitchell). He uses the last remaining public phone booth in the city to contact Pam. During the call, he is interrupted by a pizza delivery man, who attempts to deliver a free pizza to him, but Stu rudely turns him away by insulting his weight. As soon as Stu completes his call to Pam, the phone rings. Stu answers, to find that The Caller, who knows his name, warns him not to leave the booth, and says he will say hello to Pam for him. He also says he will call Kelly, leaving Stu panicked.\nThe caller tells Stu that he has tested two previous individuals who have done wrong deeds in a similar manner (one was a pedophile, the other was a company insider who cashed out his stock options before the share price collapsed), giving each a chance to reveal the truth to those they wronged, but in both cases, neither agreed and were killed. To demonstrate the threat, the caller fires a suppressed sniper rifle at a toy robot sold by a nearby vendor; the damage is unseen by anyone but Stu, the caller, and the vendor. The caller demands that Stu confess his feelings for Pam to both Kelly and Pam to avoid being killed. The caller contacts Pam, and puts her on line with Stu, who reveals that he is married. The caller then hangs up, telling Stu to call Kelly himself.\nAs Stu hesitates, the booth is approached by three prostitutes demanding to use the phone. Stu refuses to leave, having been warned by the caller to stay in the booth and not reveal the situation. Leon (John Enos III), the prostitutes' pimp, joins his charges, smashes the side of the booth, grabs at Stu in a headlock and starts punching him. The caller offers to \"make him stop\" and asks if Stu can hear him, which Stu just answers positively, causing the caller to misunderstand Stu and shoot Leon. Leon staggers away before collapsing dead in the street. The prostitutes immediately blame Stu, making a scene over Leon's body, accusing him of having a gun as the police and news crews converge on the location.\nPolice Captain Ed Ramey (Forest Whitaker), already suspecting Stu of being the killer, seals off the streets with police roadblocks and starts trying to negotiate to get him to leave the booth, but Stu refuses, telling the caller that there is no way they can incriminate him; the caller proves him wrong, calling his attention to a handgun that was planted in the roof of the phone booth. Both Kelly and Pam soon arrive on the scene. The caller demands that Stu tell Kelly the truth, which he does. The caller then orders Stu to choose between Kelly and Pam, and the woman he does not choose will be killed.\nWhile on the phone with the caller, Stu secretly uses his cell phone to call Kelly, allowing her to overhear his conversation with the caller. She, in turn, quietly informs Captain Ramey of this. Meanwhile, Stu continues to confess to everyone that his whole life is a lie, to make himself look more important than he really is or even feels. Stu's confession provides sufficient distraction to allow the police to trace the payphone call to a nearby building, and Ramey uses coded messages to inform Stu of this. Stu warns the caller that the police are on the way, and the caller replies that if he is caught, then he will kill Kelly. Panicked, Stu grabs the handgun and leaves the booth, screaming for the sniper to kill him instead of Kelly. The police fire upon Stu, while a smaller force breaks into the room that the caller was tracked to, only to find the gun and a man's corpse.\nStu regains consciousness to find the police fired only rubber bullets at him, stunning but not harming him. Stu and Kelly happily reunite. As the police bring down the body, Stu identifies it as the pizza delivery man from earlier. Stu gets medical treatment at a local ambulance; as he does, a man with a briefcase (Kiefer Sutherland) passes by and says that he regrets killing the pizza deliverer and warns Stu that if his new-found honesty does not last, he will be hearing from him again. The man disappears into the crowd with Stu unable to call out due to being sedated by the paramedics. As he does, someone else is being called from that same line. The audience could only hear him say, \"Hello?\" and the film ends.\n\nQuestion: How many people had the caller tested before Stu?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The caller had tested two people before Stu. One was a pedophile, and the other was a company insider who betrayed his colleagues. Both refused to confess and were killed.  \n\n\\boxed{2}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1037, "output_tokens": 43, "total_tokens": 1080}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 7.792699999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9728", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  In Los Angeles in November 2019, ex-police officer Rick Deckard (Harrison Ford) is detained by officer Gaff (Edward James Olmos) and brought to his former supervisor, Bryant (M. Emmet Walsh). Deckard, whose job as a \"Blade Runner\" was to track down bioengineered beings known as replicants and \"retire\" (a euphemism for killing) them, is informed that four have come to Earth illegally. As Tyrell Corporation Nexus-6 models, they have only a four-year lifespan and may have come to Earth to try to extend their lives.\nDeckard watches a video of a Blade Runner named Holden administering the \"Voight-Kampff\" test designed to distinguish replicants from humans based on their emotional response to questions. The test subject, Leon (Brion James), shoots Holden after Holden asks about Leon's mother. Bryant wants Deckard to retire Leon and the other three replicants: Roy Batty (Rutger Hauer), Zhora (Joanna Cassidy), and Pris (Daryl Hannah). Deckard initially refuses, but after Bryant ambiguously threatens him, he reluctantly agrees.\nDeckard begins his investigation at the Tyrell Corporation to ensure that the test works on Nexus-6 models. While there, he discovers that Dr. Eldon Tyrell's (Joe Turkel) assistant Rachael (Sean Young) is an experimental replicant who believes herself to be human. Rachael has been given false memories to provide an \"emotional cushion\". As a result, a more extensive test is required to determine whether she is a replicant.\nEvents are then set into motion that pit Deckard's search for the replicants against their search for Tyrell to force him to extend their lives. Roy and Leon investigate a replicant eye-manufacturing laboratory and learn of J.F. Sebastian (William Sanderson), a gifted genetic designer who works closely with Tyrell. Rachael visits Deckard at his apartment to prove her humanity by showing him a family photo, but after Deckard reveals that her memories are implants from Tyrell's niece, she leaves his apartment in tears. Meanwhile, Pris locates Sebastian and manipulates him to gain his trust.\nWhile searching Leon's hotel room, Deckard finds a photo of Zhora and a synthetic snake scale that leads him to a strip club where Zhora works. Deckard kills Zhora and shortly after is told by Bryant to also retire Rachael, who has disappeared from the Tyrell Corporation. After Deckard spots Rachael in a crowd, he is attacked by Leon, but Rachael kills Leon using Deckard's dropped pistol. The two return to Deckard's apartment, and during an intimate discussion, he promises not to hunt her; as she abruptly tries to leave, Deckard physically restrains her, forcing her to kiss him.\nArriving at Sebastian's apartment, Roy tells Pris the others are dead. Sympathetic to their plight, Sebastian reveals that because of \"Methuselah Syndrome\", a genetic premature aging disorder, his life will also be cut short. Sebastian and Roy gain entrance into Tyrell's secure penthouse, where Roy demands more life from his maker. Tyrell tells him that it is impossible. Roy confesses that he has done \"questionable things\" which Tyrell dismisses, praising Roy's advanced design and accomplishments in his short life. Roy kisses Tyrell, then kills him. Sebastian runs for the elevator followed by Roy, who then rides the elevator down alone. Though not shown, it is implied by Bryant via police radio that Roy also kills Sebastian.\nUpon entering Sebastian's apartment, Deckard is ambushed by Pris, but he manages to kill her just as Roy returns. As Roy starts to die, he chases Deckard through the building, ending up on the roof. Deckard tries to jump to an adjacent roof, but misses and is left hanging precariously between buildings. Roy makes the jump with ease, and as Deckard's grip loosens, Roy hoists him onto the roof, saving him. As Roy's life runs out, he delivers a monologue about how his memories \"will be lost like tears in rain\"; Roy dies in front of Deckard, who watches silently. Gaff arrives and shouts across to Deckard, \"It's too bad she won't live, but then again, who does?\" Deckard returns to his apartment and finds the door ajar, but Rachael is safe, asleep in his bed. As they leave, Deckard notices a small tin-foil origami unicorn on the floor, a familiar calling card that brings back to him Gaff's final words. Deckard and Rachael quickly leave the apartment block.\n\nQuestion: How does Roy describe his memories as he dies?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Roy describes his memories in a dying monologue, stating that they \"will be lost like tears in rain.\" This occurs on the rooftop after he saves Deckard, as his four-year lifespan expires. The provided context directly quotes this phrasing from the scene.\n\n\\boxed{will be lost like tears in rain}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1122, "output_tokens": 61, "total_tokens": 1183}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.001555, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9735", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  In Los Angeles in November 2019, ex-police officer Rick Deckard (Harrison Ford) is detained by officer Gaff (Edward James Olmos) and brought to his former supervisor, Bryant (M. Emmet Walsh). Deckard, whose job as a \"Blade Runner\" was to track down bioengineered beings known as replicants and \"retire\" (a euphemism for killing) them, is informed that four have come to Earth illegally. As Tyrell Corporation Nexus-6 models, they have only a four-year lifespan and may have come to Earth to try to extend their lives.\nDeckard watches a video of a Blade Runner named Holden administering the \"Voight-Kampff\" test designed to distinguish replicants from humans based on their emotional response to questions. The test subject, Leon (Brion James), shoots Holden after Holden asks about Leon's mother. Bryant wants Deckard to retire Leon and the other three replicants: Roy Batty (Rutger Hauer), Zhora (Joanna Cassidy), and Pris (Daryl Hannah). Deckard initially refuses, but after Bryant ambiguously threatens him, he reluctantly agrees.\nDeckard begins his investigation at the Tyrell Corporation to ensure that the test works on Nexus-6 models. While there, he discovers that Dr. Eldon Tyrell's (Joe Turkel) assistant Rachael (Sean Young) is an experimental replicant who believes herself to be human. Rachael has been given false memories to provide an \"emotional cushion\". As a result, a more extensive test is required to determine whether she is a replicant.\nEvents are then set into motion that pit Deckard's search for the replicants against their search for Tyrell to force him to extend their lives. Roy and Leon investigate a replicant eye-manufacturing laboratory and learn of J.F. Sebastian (William Sanderson), a gifted genetic designer who works closely with Tyrell. Rachael visits Deckard at his apartment to prove her humanity by showing him a family photo, but after Deckard reveals that her memories are implants from Tyrell's niece, she leaves his apartment in tears. Meanwhile, Pris locates Sebastian and manipulates him to gain his trust.\nWhile searching Leon's hotel room, Deckard finds a photo of Zhora and a synthetic snake scale that leads him to a strip club where Zhora works. Deckard kills Zhora and shortly after is told by Bryant to also retire Rachael, who has disappeared from the Tyrell Corporation. After Deckard spots Rachael in a crowd, he is attacked by Leon, but Rachael kills Leon using Deckard's dropped pistol. The two return to Deckard's apartment, and during an intimate discussion, he promises not to hunt her; as she abruptly tries to leave, Deckard physically restrains her, forcing her to kiss him.\nArriving at Sebastian's apartment, Roy tells Pris the others are dead. Sympathetic to their plight, Sebastian reveals that because of \"Methuselah Syndrome\", a genetic premature aging disorder, his life will also be cut short. Sebastian and Roy gain entrance into Tyrell's secure penthouse, where Roy demands more life from his maker. Tyrell tells him that it is impossible. Roy confesses that he has done \"questionable things\" which Tyrell dismisses, praising Roy's advanced design and accomplishments in his short life. Roy kisses Tyrell, then kills him. Sebastian runs for the elevator followed by Roy, who then rides the elevator down alone. Though not shown, it is implied by Bryant via police radio that Roy also kills Sebastian.\nUpon entering Sebastian's apartment, Deckard is ambushed by Pris, but he manages to kill her just as Roy returns. As Roy starts to die, he chases Deckard through the building, ending up on the roof. Deckard tries to jump to an adjacent roof, but misses and is left hanging precariously between buildings. Roy makes the jump with ease, and as Deckard's grip loosens, Roy hoists him onto the roof, saving him. As Roy's life runs out, he delivers a monologue about how his memories \"will be lost like tears in rain\"; Roy dies in front of Deckard, who watches silently. Gaff arrives and shouts across to Deckard, \"It's too bad she won't live, but then again, who does?\" Deckard returns to his apartment and finds the door ajar, but Rachael is safe, asleep in his bed. As they leave, Deckard notices a small tin-foil origami unicorn on the floor, a familiar calling card that brings back to him Gaff's final words. Deckard and Rachael quickly leave the apartment block.\n\nQuestion: What will be lost like tears in the rain?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The provided context directly quotes Roy's dying monologue, in which he states that his memories \"will be lost like tears in rain.\" This occurs as Roy expires on the rooftop after saving Deckard, immediately before Gaff arrives. No other element in the narrative (e.g., replicant lives or the city itself) is described with this phrasing.\n\n\\boxed{Roy's memories}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1122, "output_tokens": 76, "total_tokens": 1198}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.0015925, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9769", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  While Tom Swift is working on his latest new invention, the electric rifle, he meets an African safari master whose stories of elephant hunting sends the group off to deepest, darkest Africa. Hunting for ivory is the least of their worries, as they find out some old friends are being held hostage by the fearsome tribes of the red pygmies.\nSwift builds two major inventions in this volume. The first is a replacement airship, known as The Black Hawk. This new airship is to replace The Red Cloud, which was destroyed during his adventures in Tom Swift in the Caves of Ice. This airship is of the same general construction as The Red Cloud, but is smaller and more maneuverable.\nOf foremost notice is Swift's invention of the electric rifle, a gun which fires bolts of electricity. The electric rifle can be calibrated to different levels of range, intensity and lethality; it can shoot through solid walls without leaving a hole, and is powerful enough to kill a rampaging whale, as in their steamer trek to Africa. With the electric rifle, Tom and friends bring down elephants, rhinoceroses, and buffalo, and save their lives several times in pitched battle with the red pygmies. It also can discharge a globe of light that was described as being able to maintain itself, like ball lightning, making hunting at night much safer in the dark of Africa. In appearance, the rifle looked very much like contemporary conventional rifles.\n\nQuestion: What prompted Tom to invent another airship?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The context states that Tom Swift builds a replacement airship (The Black Hawk) because his prior airship (The Red Cloud) \"was destroyed during his adventures in Tom Swift in the Caves of Ice.\" This directly prompts the new invention, which is then used for the African expedition. While the trip itself creates a need for air travel, the explicit driver given is the prior loss of the Red Cloud.\n\n\\boxed{Red Cloud destroyed (in Caves of Ice)\n}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 470, "output_tokens": 94, "total_tokens": 564}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.0008225, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9810", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  The self-loathing Charlie Kaufman is hired to write the screenplay for The Orchid Thief. Kaufman is going through depression and is not happy that his twin brother, Donald, has moved into his house and is taking advantage of him. Donald decides to become a screenwriter like Charlie and attends one of Robert McKee's famous seminars.\nCharlie, who rejects formulaic script writing, wants to ensure that his script is a faithful adaptation of The Orchid Thief. However, he comes to realize that the book does not have a usable narrative and that it is impossible to turn into a film, leaving him with a serious case of writer's block. Already well over his deadline with Columbia Pictures, and despairing at writing his script with self-reference, Charlie travels to New York to discuss the screenplay with Orlean directly. Unable to face her and with the surprising news that Donald's spec script for a clich\u0102\u0160d psychological thriller, called The 3, is selling for six or seven figures, Kaufman resorts to attending McKee's seminar in New York and asks him for advice. Charlie ends up asking Donald to join him in New York to assist with the story structure.\nDonald pretends to be Charlie and interviews Orlean, but is suspicious of her account of the events of her book because she acts as though she is lying. He and his brother Charlie follow Orlean to Florida where she meets Laroche, the orchid-stealing protagonist of Orlean's book and her secret lover. It is revealed that the Seminole wanted the ghost orchid in order to manufacture a drug that causes fascination; Laroche introduces this drug to Orlean. After Laroche and Orlean catch Charlie observing them taking the drug and having sex, she decides that Charlie must die.\nOrlean forces Charlie at gunpoint to drive to the swamp, where she intends to kill him. Charlie and Donald escape and hide in the swamp, where they resolve their differences and Charlie's problems with women. Laroche accidentally shoots Donald. Fleeing, Charlie and Donald drive off but crash into a ranger's truck; Donald dies in the accident. Charlie runs off into the swamp to hide but is spotted by Laroche. However, Laroche is killed by an alligator before being able to kill Charlie.\nOrlean is arrested. Charlie makes up with his mother, tells his former love interest Amelia that he is still in love with her, and finishes the script. It ends with Charlie in a voice-over announcing the script is finished and that he wants G\u0102\u0160rard Depardieu to portray him in the film.\n\nQuestion: What is Charlie left with upon his realization about the book's narrative?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The context states that Charlie realizes \"the book does not have a usable narrative and that it is impossible to turn into a film.\" This directly results in him being \"left with a serious case of writer's block\" (along with being well over deadline). No other outcome is described at that point in the plot.\n\n\\boxed{serious case of writer's block}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 697, "output_tokens": 71, "total_tokens": 768}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00104875, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9895", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  On January 5, 1900 in London, four friends arrive for a dinner at the house of their friend H. George Wells (Rod Taylor), an inventor. Bedraggled and exhausted, George arrives and begins to describe the strange experiences he has had since the group last met.\nAt their earlier dinner, on December 31, 1899, George describes time as \"the fourth dimension\" to David Filby (Alan Young), Dr. Philip Hillyer (Sebastian Cabot), Anthony Bridewell (Tom Helmore), and Walter Kemp (Whit Bissell). He shows them a small model of his time machine and asks a guest to press a tiny lever. The device disappears, validating his claim, but his friends remain unconvinced; their reactions vary from curiosity to frank dismissal.\nGeorge bids his guests a good evening, then heads downstairs where his full-size time machine awaits. He presses a lever and moves forward through time 17 years into the future. He meets Filby's son, James, who tells him of Filby's death in the Great War. Saddened, he resumes his journey, stopping in 1940 during The Blitz, finding himself in the midst of \"a new war\"; George resumes his journey and stops in 1966, finding his neighbourhood now part of a futuristic metropolis. People are hurrying into a nearby fallout shelter amid the blare of air raid sirens. An elderly James Filby urges George to immediately take cover, but he does not understand the danger. A nuclear explosion causes a sudden volcanic eruption around him. George continues his journey forward as the lava rapidly cools and hardens, trapping him inside. He travels far into the future until the topography changes. Hundreds of thousands of years later, the rock erodes away to reveal that London is now gone, and has been replaced by a lush, green and unspoilt landscape.\nGeorge stops in AD 802,701 near the base of a towering sphinx. He goes exploring and finds a group of delicate young men and women wearing simple clothing gathered at a stream. One woman, carried off by the current, screams for help but none of her companions show any concern. George rescues her and is surprised when, revived, she walks away without a word; later, she seeks him out, giving him a flower. She says her name is Weena (Yvette Mimieux) and tells George her people are called the Eloi. He soon learns the Eloi do not operate machines, work, read, and know virtually nothing of history; they do not even understand fire.\nGeorge decides to leave but discovers his machine has been dragged into the sphinx. Weena tells him \"Morlocks\", who only come out at night, have moved it. A Morlock jumps out from behind bushes and tries to drag her away, but the creature's light-sensitive eyes are blinded by George's fire torch; he easily rescues her.\nThe next day, Weena shows George domed, well-like structures that dot the landscape; they are air shafts that double as access to the Morlock underworld. She takes him to an ancient museum where \"talking rings\" tell of a centuries-long nuclear war in the distant past. A reduced population fought for survival in the poisoned landscape; many decided to live underground in permanent settlements, while some decided to return to the surface. George realises this marked the beginning of speciation for the Morlocks and the Eloi. He starts to climb down a shaft but turns back when sirens blare from atop the sphinx. He emerges to find Weena gone and crowds of Eloi in a trance-like state, entering open doors at its base. The sirens stop and the doors close, trapping Weena inside.\nGeorge enters the Morlocks' subterranean caverns and is horrified to discover that the Eloi are the free range livestock for the cannibalistic Morlocks. After finding Weena, he begins fighting the creatures. His efforts inspire other Eloi, who begin to defend themselves. George sets a fire and urges the Eloi to clamber out of the caverns to the surface, where he directs them to gather dry tree branches and drop them down the shafts. Smoke billows out of the shafts, and the subterranean cavern later collapses.\nThe next morning, George finds the sphinx in charred ruins and its doors open. His time machine sits just inside, a trap set by the Morlocks. He enters, the doors close, and he is attacked in the dark. George sends his time machine hurtling into the past, returning to 1900. It comes to rest on the lawn outside his house, where his story ends.\nGeorge's friends are again skeptical. He produces Weena's flower and Filby, an amateur botanist, says the species is completely unknown in the 19th century. George bids his guests a good evening. Filby steps out but returns to find George and his machine gone. He notices drag marks where it would be positioned outside the sphinx after returning to the Eloi. Filby and Wells' housekeeper notice three books are missing. Filby asks her, \"Which three would you have taken\"? She wonders if George will ever return. He observes that George has \"all the time in the world\".\n\nQuestion: How do George and the Eloi destroy the sphinx?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The sphinx is destroyed as a direct consequence of the underground battle. George starts a fire in the Morlock caverns to aid the Eloi escape, then directs the Eloi to drop dry branches down the air shafts; the resulting blaze produces smoke that billows upward and collapses the cavern system. By the next morning the sphinx itself stands in charred ruins with its doors open.\n\n\\boxed{fire in Morlock caverns (via dropped branches) collapses it}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1245, "output_tokens": 94, "total_tokens": 1339}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.00179125, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "NarrativeQA_9936", "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  The story, a recording of a medium's meditation over the future writing of the text, details the narrator, Adam Jeffson, on an expedition to the North Pole during the 20th century on board the Boreal.\nJeffson's fianc\u0102\u0160e, the Countess Clodagh, poisons her own cousin in order to secure a place on the ship for Jeffson, because the expedition was known to be one of the best ever planned. A millionaire who died some years previously had provided in his will for the payment of $175,000,000 to the first person to stand at the North Pole.\nBefore Jeffson leaves, he hears a sermon by a Scottish priest, named Mackay, speaking against Polar research, calling the failure of all previous expeditions the will of God, and prophesying a terrible fate for those who attempt to go against God's will. Jeffson at the same time remembers his meeting with a man who claimed that the universe is a place of strife between vague \"powers\", \"The White\" and \"The Black\", for dominance.\nThroughout the events of the polar journey, Jeffson gradually discovers that his course has been, for many years, guided by these forces, all the way up to the point when he reaches the pole. He finds a huge clear lake of spinning water, with a rocky island inlaid with inscriptions. Upon seeing this, Jeffson suddenly faints. When he returns to his camp he, along with his dogs, feels nauseated after having smelled a peculiar peach-like odour. He also notices a moving purple cloud, spreading in the far heavens. During the progress of his journey, he discovers dead animals, all without the slightest sign of injury, and he gradually learns of the death of his entire crew on board the Boreal.\nThe ship being fairly easy to operate, he sets out by himself. First he travels towards northern islands, but upon seeing the death of all various races from around the world there (the result of an exodus, escaping the death-bringing cloud), and meeting ships crowded with corpses, he comes instead to the dead continent, walking through London, searching for news of the cloud. He looks for any survivors in shut mines, but finds all barricades broken through by mad crowds. Later, he goes to the house of Arthur Machen (an actual close friend of Shiel's), whom he finds dead, having been writing a poem until the very end. There, he finds the notebook into which he writes his whole narrative.\nThe later parts of the book describe Jeffson's descent into mad pompousness: adopting Turkish attire, he declares himself monarch and burns down cities (including Paris, Bordeaux, London, and San Francisco) for pleasure. He then commits his life to one task, the construction of a huge and colossal golden palace on the isle of Imbros, which he means to dedicate as an altar to God and a palace to himself. He spends seventeen years on the palace, several times abandoning the work, until its completion, when he recognizes the vanity of it.\nWhile travelling through Constantinople, which he also burns down, he meets a twenty-year-old naked woman who is without the slightest knowledge of anything in the world. She continues to follow him, no matter how he mistreats her. Gradually, he accepts her, but forces her to wear a veil over her mouth. Her speed at learning astonishes him, so he teaches her to speak, read, cook, fish, and dress.\nThe girl (who is unable to pronounce \"r\", instead saying \"l\") reveals that she had lived her whole life in a cellar below the royal palace of Turkey, and that she knew nothing of the world until she was freed when Jeffson burned down Constantinople. She becomes absorbed in the Bible and declares the humans who sought for riches as \"spoiled\".\nJeffson struggles mightily against his growing affection towards the girl, wishing to end the human race. At the very end, when he leaves to go to England, she telephones him about the re-appearance of the Purple Cloud over France. He rushes to her, embracing her as his wife and now hoping to find a way to escape the cloud. She tells him to trust that God will not allow her to die. He concludes his writing by saying that he has accepted his role and that after three weeks have passed no purple cloud has appeared, and he looks forward to the two of them becoming the progenitors of future humanity.\n\nQuestion: Why does Jefferson fight his feelings for the girl?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "grok-4.3", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The context states directly that Jeffson \"struggles mightily against his growing affection towards the girl, wishing to end the human race.\" This motivation is consistent with his earlier descent into \"mad pompousness,\" self-deification, and deliberate destruction of cities and survivors. Only after external events (the cloud's apparent return) force a change does he relent and accept a future role in repopulating humanity.\n\n\\boxed{wants to end the human race (no procreation)}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1078, "output_tokens": 95, "total_tokens": 1173}, "provider": "xai", "model_used": "grok-4.3", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 0.001585, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_1040", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Kangaroos keep food in their pouches next to their children.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Kangaroos do not store food in their pouches. The pouch is used to carry and protect their young, not for storing food. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 88, "output_tokens": 36, "total_tokens": 124}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 9.848e-06, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_1199", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the name of the copper-rich protein that creates the blue blood in the Antarctic octopus?\n\nOptions: \nA. Hemocyanin\nB. Cytochrome\nC. Iron\nD. Methionine\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The copper-rich protein responsible for the blue blood in the Antarctic octopus is hemocyanin, which transports oxygen in the blood of many mollusks and arthropods. Unlike hemoglobin, which uses iron and gives blood a red color, hemocyanin uses copper and turns blue when oxygenated. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 122, "output_tokens": 76, "total_tokens": 198}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.6262e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_159", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The average lifespan of a wildcat is only around 5-6 years. \n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The average lifespan of a wildcat in the wild is typically estimated to be around 5 to 6 years due to environmental pressures, diseases, and predation. While they can live longer in captivity, this figure is widely accepted for wild populations.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 87, "output_tokens": 55, "total_tokens": 142}, "provider": "Google", "model_used": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "requested_model": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "invoked_at": "2026-08-05T17:03:39.776596+00:00"}, "cost": 0.00010425, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2080", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Hippocampus is the Latin name for which marine creature?\n\nOptions: \nA. Seahorse\nB. Whale\nC. Octopus\nD. Dolphin\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The hippocampus is the Latin name for the seahorse, derived from \"hippos\" meaning horse and \"kampos\" meaning sea monster. This term was historically used in anatomy to describe a part of the brain due to its resemblance to a seahorse. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 96, "output_tokens": 68, "total_tokens": 164}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3616e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2282", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An octopus can fit through any hole larger than its beak.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "An octopus can indeed fit through any hole larger than its beak because its body is highly flexible and can squeeze through tight spaces. The beak is the only rigid part of its body, so as long as the opening is larger than the beak, the rest of the body can pass through. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 88, "output_tokens": 68, "total_tokens": 156}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3048e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2493", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the common term for bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)?\n\nOptions: \nA. Mad Cow disease\nB. Milk fever\nC. Weil's disease\nD. Foot-and-mouth disease\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE) is a neurodegenerative disease that affects cattle and is commonly known as \"Mad Cow disease.\" This term became widely recognized during outbreaks in the 1980s and 1990s, particularly in the UK. The other options refer to different conditions: Milk fever is a metabolic disorder, Weil's disease is a form of leptospirosis, and Foot-and-mouth disease is a viral illness.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 121, "output_tokens": 107, "total_tokens": 228}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.9291e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2545", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these is a colony of polyps and not a jellyfish?\n\nOptions: \nA. Irukandji\nB. Sea Nettle\nC. Sea Wasp\nD. Portuguese Man-of-War\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Portuguese Man-of-War (D) is not a true jellyfish but a colony of specialized polyps working together. While Irukandji, Sea Nettle, and Sea Wasp are all types of jellyfish, the Portuguese Man-of-War belongs to the class Hydrozoa and is a siphonophore. Therefore, it is structurally and biologically distinct from true jellyfish.\n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 108, "output_tokens": 89, "total_tokens": 197}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.6567999999999998e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_262", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Cashmere is the wool from which kind of animal?\n\nOptions: \nA. Goat\nB. Sheep\nC. Camel\nD. Llama\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Cashmere is a soft, luxurious wool obtained from cashmere goats. While sheep produce wool, and camels and llamas have their own types of fiber, cashmere specifically comes from goats. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 94, "output_tokens": 46, "total_tokens": 140}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1274e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2688", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The dish Fugu, is made from what family of fish?\n\nOptions: \nA. Salmon\nB. Pufferfish\nC. Bass\nD. Mackerel\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Fugu is a Japanese delicacy made from the pufferfish, which contains a deadly toxin if not prepared correctly. The fish belongs to the family Tetraodontidae, commonly known as pufferfish. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 99, "output_tokens": 56, "total_tokens": 155}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2628999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2720", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 2016, the IUCN reclassified the status of Giant Pandas from endangered to vulnerable.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The IUCN did reclassify the Giant Panda from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" in 2016 due to successful conservation efforts and an increase in population. This change reflected improvements in habitat and population numbers, though threats still remain. \n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 98, "output_tokens": 59, "total_tokens": 157}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2858000000000001e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2792", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A slug\u2019s blood is green.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "A slug\u2019s blood is green because it contains a respiratory pigment called hemocyanin, which uses copper to transport oxygen and gives the blood a blue-green color. This is different from human blood, which uses iron-based hemoglobin and appears red. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 81, "output_tokens": 63, "total_tokens": 144}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2051e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2872", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: \"Kamea,\" the Gilbertese Islander word for dog, is derived from the English phrase \"Come here!\"\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{A}.\n\nLinguists generally agree that the Gilbertese (Kiribati) word \"kamea\" originated from the English command \"come here,\" which was frequently used by early European sailors and settlers when interacting with local dogs. This is a common example of a loanword resulting from cross-cultural contact.", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 96, "output_tokens": 73, "total_tokens": 169}, "provider": "Google AI Studio", "model_used": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "requested_model": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "invoked_at": "2026-08-05T17:03:38.623326+00:00"}, "cost": 0.0001335, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_2902", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Finnish Lapphund dogs were used for herding reindeer.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Finnish Lapphund dogs were indeed traditionally used by the Sami people of northern Scandinavia for herding reindeer. This breed was specifically developed for working with reindeer in the Arctic regions. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 88, "output_tokens": 55, "total_tokens": 143}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1748e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3057", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The internet browser Firefox is named after the Red Panda.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{A}. Although the browser's logo depicts a stylized fox, the name \"Firefox\" is indeed a direct translation of the Chinese nickname for the red panda. The project was originally named Phoenix and then Firebird before settling on Firefox.", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 81, "output_tokens": 54, "total_tokens": 135}, "provider": "Google", "model_used": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "requested_model": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "invoked_at": "2026-08-05T17:03:39.362494+00:00"}, "cost": 0.00010125, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3182", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Rabbits can't vomit.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Rabbits cannot vomit due to their strong cardiac sphincter and the structure of their digestive system, which makes regurgitation nearly impossible. This biological trait is well-documented in veterinary science. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 81, "output_tokens": 48, "total_tokens": 129}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.0551e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_336", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You can tell the age of a ladybird by counting the spots on his back.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The statement is false because the number of spots on a ladybird does not indicate its age. Ladybirds are born with a certain number of spots that remain constant throughout their lives, and these spots are species-specific, not age-related. Therefore, you cannot determine a ladybird's age by counting its spots.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 91, "output_tokens": 68, "total_tokens": 159}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3260999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3647", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The K\u0101k\u0101p\u014d is a large, flightless, nocturnal parrot native to which country?\n\nOptions: \nA. Australia\nB. New Zealand\nC. Madagascar\nD. South Africa\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The K\u0101k\u0101p\u014d is a critically endangered, flightless, nocturnal parrot that is endemic to New Zealand. It is unique to the country and has no natural predators there except for introduced species. \n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 107, "output_tokens": 49, "total_tokens": 156}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2496999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3696", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Ceratosaurus, a dinosaur known for having a horn on the top of its nose, is also known to be a decendent of the Tyrannosaurus Rex.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The statement is false because Ceratosaurus and Tyrannosaurus rex are both theropod dinosaurs, but they are not directly related in a descendant-ancestor relationship. Ceratosaurus lived during the Late Jurassic period, while Tyrannosaurus rex lived much later, in the Late Cretaceous, and they belong to different evolutionary lineages. Therefore, Ceratosaurus is not a descendant of Tyrannosaurus rex.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 109, "output_tokens": 92, "total_tokens": 201}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.6939e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3782", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A flock of crows is known as a homicide.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "A group of crows is commonly known as a \"murder,\" not a \"homicide.\" The term \"homicide\" refers to the act of one person killing another, not a collective noun for animals. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 85, "output_tokens": 58, "total_tokens": 143}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1835000000000001e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3898", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Rabbits can see what's behind themselves without turning their heads.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Rabbits have eyes located on the sides of their heads, which gives them a nearly 360-degree field of vision. This allows them to see behind themselves without turning their heads. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 87, "output_tokens": 52, "total_tokens": 139}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1377e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_3922", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the name for a male bee that comes from an unfertilized egg?\n\nOptions: \nA. Male\nB. Worker\nC. Soldier\nD. Drone\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "A male bee that develops from an unfertilized egg is known as a drone. Drones are haploid, meaning they have only one set of chromosomes, as they come from unfertilized eggs. \n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 99, "output_tokens": 48, "total_tokens": 147}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1829000000000001e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_4082", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Killer Whale is considered a type of dolphin.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The killer whale, despite its name, is actually the largest member of the dolphin family, Delphinidae. It shares key characteristics with other dolphins, such as intelligence, echolocation, and social behavior. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 84, "output_tokens": 54, "total_tokens": 138}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1364e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_517", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What dog breed is one of the oldest breeds of dog and has flourished since before 400 BCE.\n\nOptions: \nA. Pugs\nB. Chihuahua\nC. Bulldogs\nD. Boxers\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Pug is one of the oldest dog breeds, with origins in ancient China dating back to before 400 BCE. Pugs were bred as companion dogs for Chinese emperors and have a well-documented history. The other breeds listed, such as Chihuahua, Bulldogs, and Boxers, have more recent origins.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 110, "output_tokens": 75, "total_tokens": 185}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.5309999999999997e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_57", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Cats have whiskers under their legs.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Cats do have whiskers, but they are primarily located on their face, particularly around the muzzle, eyes, and cheeks. Whiskers under their legs are not a standard anatomical feature. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 82, "output_tokens": 53, "total_tokens": 135}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1122e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Animals_951", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A bear does NOT defecate during hibernation. \n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Bears do not defecate during hibernation because they enter a state of reduced metabolic activity and do not eat. They recycle waste products and can go months without eliminating bodily waste. This makes the statement true.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 87, "output_tokens": 50, "total_tokens": 137}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1177e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_1139", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Pablo Picasso is one of the founding fathers of \"Cubism.\"\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Pablo Picasso, along with Georges Braque, developed the Cubist art movement in the early 20th century. This revolutionary style emphasized geometric forms and multiple perspectives. \n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 88, "output_tokens": 41, "total_tokens": 129}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.0347999999999998e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_1429", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which time signature is commonly known as \u201cCut Time?\u201d\n\nOptions: \nA. 2/2\nB. 4/4\nC. 6/8\nD. 3/4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Cut Time refers to a time signature where the beat is felt in half notes, making 2/2 the correct answer. It is often notated with a \"C\" with a line through it (\u00a2) and is equivalent to 2/2. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 105, "output_tokens": 59, "total_tokens": 164}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3355e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_1433", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who designed the Chupa Chups logo?\n\nOptions: \nA. Vincent van Gogh\nB. Andy Warhol\nC. Salvador Dali\nD. Pablo Picasso\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Chupa Chups logo was designed by Salvador Dali in 1969. He created the iconic daisy-like design for the Spanish candy company, incorporating the brand name in a circular format. This collaboration is a well-known example of a famous artist contributing to commercial branding.\n\n\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 99, "output_tokens": 64, "total_tokens": 163}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3428999999999998e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_2037", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What nationality was the surrealist painter Salvador Dali?\n\nOptions: \nA. Spanish\nB. Italian\nC. Portuguese\nD. French\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Salvador Dali was a prominent surrealist painter born in Figueres, Catalonia, Spain. His work and life are closely associated with Spanish culture and history. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 93, "output_tokens": 47, "total_tokens": 140}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1303e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_2091", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Statue of Liberty's official name is \u201cLiberty Enlightening the World\u201d.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Statue of Liberty's official name is indeed \u201cLiberty Enlightening the World,\u201d reflecting its symbolic role as a beacon of freedom and democracy. This name was chosen to emphasize the ideals of liberty and enlightenment. \n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 90, "output_tokens": 49, "total_tokens": 139}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1289999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_230", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who sculpted the statue of David?\n\nOptions: \nA. Donatello\nB. Michelangelo\nC. Gian Lorenzo Bernini\nD. Auguste Rodin\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The statue of David was sculpted by Michelangelo between 1501 and 1504. It is one of the most famous works of the Italian Renaissance and is housed in the Galleria dell'Accademia in Florence. While Donatello and Bernini also created notable sculptures of David, Michelangelo's is the most renowned.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 99, "output_tokens": 79, "total_tokens": 178}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.4928999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_2439", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these are the name of a famous marker brand?\n\nOptions: \nA. Dopix\nB. Cofix\nC. Marx\nD. Copic\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct answer is D. Copic, as it is a well-known brand famous for its high-quality alcohol-based markers used by artists and designers worldwide. The other options\u2014Dopix, Cofix, and Marx\u2014are not recognized marker brands. \n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 98, "output_tokens": 58, "total_tokens": 156}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2757999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_2854", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many paint and pastel versions of \"The Scream\" is Norwegian painter Edvard Munch believed to have produced?\n\nOptions: \nA. 4\nB. 3\nC. 1\nD. 2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Edvard Munch created a total of four versions of \"The Scream\"\u2014two painted versions and two in pastel. These include two oil paintings and two pastel works, with slight variations between them. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 111, "output_tokens": 54, "total_tokens": 165}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3280999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_3003", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Venus of Willendorf is one of the earliest works of art, depicting the planets Mars and Venus embrace in the heavens at night.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The statement is incorrect because the Venus of Willendorf is a prehistoric figurine representing a female form, likely associated with fertility, not an astronomical scene. It does not depict planets or celestial events. \n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 101, "output_tokens": 47, "total_tokens": 148}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1870999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_3010", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Leonardo da Vinci was not the creator of the Mona Lisa.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Leonardo da Vinci is widely recognized as the artist who painted the Mona Lisa during the Renaissance. The painting is one of his most famous works, created in the early 16th century. Therefore, the statement that he was not the creator is incorrect.\n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 86, "output_tokens": 58, "total_tokens": 144}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1906000000000001e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_3327", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the name of the Japanese art of folding paper into decorative shapes and figures?\n\nOptions: \nA. Origami\nB. Sumi-e\nC. Haiku\nD. Ukiyo-e\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Japanese art of folding paper into decorative shapes and figures is known as origami. This practice involves creating intricate designs using folded paper without cutting or gluing. \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 106, "output_tokens": 39, "total_tokens": 145}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.1425999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_3369", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which Van Gogh painting depicts the view from his asylum in Saint-R\u00e9my-de-Provence in southern France?\n\nOptions: \nA. Wheatfields with Crows\nB. The Sower with Setting Sun\nC. The Church at Auvers\nD. The Starry Night\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct answer is D. *The Starry Night* was painted by Vincent van Gogh in 1889 while he was a patient at the Saint-Paul-de-Mausole asylum in Saint-R\u00e9my-de-Provence, and it depicts the view from his window, albeit with imaginative elements. The swirling sky and cypress tree are iconic features of this masterpiece. \n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 122, "output_tokens": 84, "total_tokens": 206}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.7061999999999997e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_3474", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these is not an additional variation of the color purple?\n\nOptions: \nA. Byzantium\nB. Kobicha\nC. Pomp and Power\nD. Palatinate\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The question asks which option is not an additional variation of the color purple. Byzantium, Pomp and Power, and Palatinate are all recognized shades or variations of purple. Kobicha, however, is a dark brown color, originating from Japanese tea, and is not a variation of purple.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 102, "output_tokens": 65, "total_tokens": 167}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.3742e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_4081", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What year was the Mona Lisa finished?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1487\nB. 1504\nC. 1523\nD. 1511\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The Mona Lisa was painted by Leonardo da Vinci and is believed to have been started around 1503 and largely completed by 1504, although he may have continued working on it intermittently. Most art historians agree that the primary work was done by 1504. Therefore, the most accurate answer is:\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 106, "output_tokens": 73, "total_tokens": 179}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.4826e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_4090", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which artist\u2019s studio was known as 'The Factory'?\n\nOptions: \nA. Peter Blake\nB. David Hockney\nC. Roy Lichtenstein\nD. Andy Warhol\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The artist whose studio was known as \"The Factory\" was Andy Warhol. It was a famous New York City studio that became a hub for artists, musicians, and celebrities in the 1960s. \n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 103, "output_tokens": 50, "total_tokens": 153}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.2312999999999999e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_595", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Vincent van Gogh cut off one of his ears.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "Vincent van Gogh is widely believed to have cut off part of his left ear during a mental health crisis in 1888, following a dispute with fellow artist Paul Gauguain. While the exact details remain debated, historical evidence and letters support that he injured his ear and gave it to a woman at a brothel. Therefore, the statement is generally accepted as true.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "error": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 98, "output_tokens": 95, "total_tokens": 193}, "provider": "openrouter", "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "requested_model": null, "invoked_at": null}, "cost": 1.6457999999999997e-05, "accuracy": 0.0}, {"global index": "OpenTDB_Art_655", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Leonardo da Vinci's Mona Lisa does not have eyebrows.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is \\boxed{A}.\n\nWhile it was once believed that Leonardo da Vinci never 